On Postgres, if a database cache lock is already taken and you try to get it inside a transaction, the whole transaction fails. You don't need to call `Cache::lock()` yourself for this, dispatching a unique job is enough:

```php
DB::transaction(function () use ($invoice) {
    $invoice->update([...]);

    SyncInvoice::dispatch($invoice->id); // ShouldBeUnique, already queued
});
```

```
SQLSTATE[25P02]: In failed sql transaction: current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block
```

The lock tries an insert first and does an update if the key exists. On Postgres the failed insert aborts the transaction, so the update fails too.

This was mentioned in #36280. Later the same problem in `DatabaseStore::add()` was fixed in #49025 by using `insertOrIgnore()`. This does the same for the lock.

Lychee works around this by replacing the whole database cache driver: https://github.com/LycheeOrg/Lychee/blob/master/app/Services/Cache/TransactionSafeDatabaseLock.php

